---
functor: 1
title: Operations
---

set heading(numbering: none)

Functor has 35 operations in 9 families. Write an operation after a dot: `theta.hat`. Each operation has a meaning. The meaning is a guideline, not a rule.

# decorate

A mark above or below the base. The mark follows the slant of an italic letter.

| Operation | Example | Meaning |
|:--|:-:|:--|
| [`hat`](symbols/ops/hat.html) | $x.hat$ | a derived object, often an estimate |
| [`tilde`](symbols/ops/tilde.html) | $x.tilde$ | a related object, often an approximation |
| [`bar`](symbols/ops/bar.html) | $x.bar$ | a derived object, often a mean or conjugate |
| [`dot`](symbols/ops/dot.html) | $x.dot$ | a first derivative in time |
| [`ddot`](symbols/ops/ddot.html) | $x.ddot$ | a second derivative in time |
| [`check`](symbols/ops/check.html) | $f.check$ | a dual or inverse object |
| [`breve`](symbols/ops/breve.html) | $u.breve$ | a related object |
| [`arrow`](symbols/ops/arrow.html) | $v.arrow$ | a vector |
| [`under`](symbols/ops/under.html) | $x.under$ | a related object |

# enclose

A shape around the base: a circle, a box or a square.

| Operation | Example | Meaning |
|:--|:-:|:--|
| [`circle`](symbols/ops/circle.html) | $sum.circle$ | a closed or restricted version |
| [`box`](symbols/ops/box.html) | $plus.box$ | a restricted or boxed version |
| [`square`](symbols/ops/square.html) | $x.square$ | a boxed version with equal sides |

# strike

A line through the base.

| Operation | Example | Meaning |
|:--|:-:|:--|
| [`not`](symbols/ops/not.html) | $in.not$ | negation |
| [`cross`](symbols/ops/cross.html) | $h.cross$ | a fixed variant |

# repeat

Copies of the base side by side. A font glyph is used when one exists, for example two integrals.

| Operation | Example | Meaning |
|:--|:-:|:--|
| [`x2`](symbols/ops/x2.html) | $integral.x2$ | second order |
| [`x3`](symbols/ops/x3.html) | $integral.x3$ | third order |
| [`x4`](symbols/ops/x4.html) | $integral.x4$ | fourth order |

# reflect

A mirror image or a half turn of the base.

| Operation | Example | Meaning |
|:--|:-:|:--|
| [`flip`](symbols/ops/flip.html) | $E.flip$ | a dual or opposite |
| [`flipv`](symbols/ops/flipv.html) | $Pi.flipv$ | a dual or opposite |
| [`turn`](symbols/ops/turn.html) | $A.turn$ | a dual or opposite |

# style

Another alphabet for a letter: bold, double-struck, fraktur, script, upright.

| Operation | Example | Meaning |
|:--|:-:|:--|
| [`bold`](symbols/ops/bold.html) | $v.bold$ | a new kind of object, often a vector |
| [`bb`](symbols/ops/bb.html) | $R.bb$ | a number set |
| [`frak`](symbols/ops/frak.html) | $g.frak$ | an ideal, an algebra or a structure |
| [`script`](symbols/ops/script.html) | $L.script$ | a family, a category or a transform |
| [`up`](symbols/ops/up.html) | $d.up$ | a constant, a unit or a name |

# magnify

A smaller or larger copy of the base.

| Operation | Example | Meaning |
|:--|:-:|:--|
| [`small`](symbols/ops/small.html) | $T.up.small$ | a smaller version, about four fifths |

# deform

A changed outline of the base. The straight parts grow and the ends and thin strokes keep their shape.

| Operation | Example | Meaning |
|:--|:-:|:--|
| [`tall`](symbols/ops/tall.html) | $integral.tall$ | a taller version; the straight parts grow and the ends keep their shape |
| [`wide`](symbols/ops/wide.html) | $to.wide$ | a wider version, such as a long arrow; the ends keep their shape |

# fence

Brackets around a group or a symbol. The brackets grow with the content.

| Operation | Example | Meaning |
|:--|:-:|:--|
| [`paren`](symbols/ops/paren.html) | $(a + b).paren$ | round brackets, a group or a tuple |
| [`brack`](symbols/ops/brack.html) | $(a, b).brack$ | square brackets, a group or a matrix |
| [`brace`](symbols/ops/brace.html) | $(x).brace$ | curly brackets, a set |
| [`angle`](symbols/ops/angle.html) | $(a + b).angle$ | angle brackets, a pairing or an average |
| [`vbar`](symbols/ops/vbar.html) | $(x).vbar$ | bars, a size or a determinant |
| [`floor`](symbols/ops/floor.html) | $(x).floor$ | round down |
| [`ceil`](symbols/ops/ceil.html) | $(x).ceil$ | round up |

# Not yet

The compiler refuses these with a clear message: `deform` (Deform has two operations: `tall` and `wide`. For example `paren.tall` or `to.wide`.), `stretch` (Write `tall` or `wide`. They stretch the straight parts and keep the ends.), `fuse` (`fuse` comes in a later phase. Use `join(a, b)` for now.).
